## P18-V01 Remoting codec public API reconciliation — 2026-10-15

- Source review: Java 5.5.1 exposes `RemotingCommand.encode/decode`,
  `NettyEncoder.encode`, and `NettyDecoder.decode`; its 16 MiB frame default is
  private and its `frameMaxLength` property applies to inbound decoding only.
- Rust public codec helpers now mirror those encoding/decoding capabilities
  without caller-selected per-call bounds. The Java default is crate-private;
  bounded helpers remain internal for the connection decoder and encoder.
  Cross-crate loopback fixture callsites now use the public no-bound API.
- Added compile-fail API guards for the old frame/header bounds and default
  constant, plus an internal unit test for bounded transport encoding/decoding.
- First focused run exposed an overly specific expected encoder error variant;
  the test now checks the bound rejection without requiring one variant.
- Code commit: `c27b60e4 fix(remoting): align public codec bounds with Java API`.
- PASS after the change: formatter, all-target/all-feature workspace Clippy,
  UTC default workspace tests, all-feature workspace tests, Maven verify
  (37 scenarios, frame probes and Java/Rust cross-decodes), differential
  self-test/manifest, race/leak smoke (16 × 3), fuzz smoke (1,824 mutations),
  release examples, benchmark compilation, metadata/package checks, UTC and
  Asia/Taipei unique-ID differential, and `git diff --check`.
- This closes the codec API disposition only. The broader P18-V01 Java/Rust
  source/API/settings/input/error/flow and reverse-export audit remains open.

